        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="true" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="false" heatedGarage="true" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" evChargingStation="false" outdoorGasAppliances="false" commonSpace="false" elevator="false" fitnessRoom="false" snowmeltSystem="false" unheatedGarage="false" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

Air leakage was observed along the top plates where the wall and ceilling connect. This was mainly observed along the livingroom and adjacent bedroom. 

Air leakage was also observed through both of the bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.

As the blower door test results indicated the air leakage is a mainly a source from larger opening, I suspect one of the main sources of air leakage is the old furnace flue. 



Savings: For a minimum 20% improvement you could potentially save $ 310 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.1 GJ.</airSeal>
                <AdditionalComments>DEPRESSURIZATION TEST

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (exhaust fans, clothes dryer, range hood, etc.), indicated a  pressure drop of 1.4 Pascal?????????s. If this number was higher than 5.0 Pascal?????????s, the use any atmospherically vented appliances may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home. You don?????????t have any atmospherically vented appliances at the time of the evaluation because your boiler is a sealed combustion unit and the domestic hot water is also prodcued from the boiler. 



Recommendation:

We strongly recommend that you ensure an up-to-date carbon monoxide (CO) detector is in your home, as well as a smoke detector, if not already present.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
